#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153365 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $a$ is an integer and that $n$ is a positive integer. Then there are *unique* integers $q$ (called the **quotient**) and $r$ (called the **remainder**) such that\n\n$$\na = qn + r \\quad\\textrm{where}\\quad 0 \\leq r \\lt n\n$$\n\nThis simple idea has far-reaching consequences, as we'll soon discover.\n\nAs an example, suppose that $a=56$ and $n=5$. Then, we can write\n\n$$\n56 = \\underbrace{{\\color{red}{11}}}_{q}\\cdot 5 + \\underbrace{{\\color{blue}{1}}}_{r}\n$$\n\nWe can find $q$ using integer division. Once we know $q$, it's then straightforward to find $r$." :tutorial/id #uuid "afc98e47-2d5e-5d68-8ebf-06da0e82b2fc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4006 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
